WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
(Furious Snapping)

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.100
The thing that we learned is that laptops, when you unplug them, everything clocks down.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.380
They dont run at full power

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.280
Running laptops by themselves on your back, not gonna work.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.820
We needed real power, real energy density.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.240
So now we got laptops, plugged in. They've been running VR when they're plugged in, flawless.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.320
Now we gotta trick the laptop into thinking its plugged in.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:19.040
[Pop]

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:19.860
Whoops

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:29.720
I'm trying to build a backpack mounted PC because I really want to play VR without wires.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:33.620
I've been trying to do this for years.
(Nick: Yeah, this sounds kinda familar)

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:35.540
Yeah, it has a desktop 980 in it

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.740
Ugh, it overheated again

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.060
Yep, its kinda familiar. Same old story.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:41.960
I didn't have the right hardware for it, and now I do.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:45.040
So I'm going to take this battery, full charge is 25. 2 volts

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.360
I'm going to step it down to that 19.5 that that laptop requires

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.480
Its a little tricky, I gotta make sure I do it right

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.320
I'm gonna run this battery into the step down converter

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:59.400
I'm going to run the step down converter through a power analyzer so I can see how much amperage is being pulled through it.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.020
And then I need to get the plug

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.160
Thats been the hardest part, the plug for the laptop.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:08.660
So this is an Origin laptop. It uses this dang plug that I can't find it.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.960
It's female pin sockets, on a male plug

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:16.880
So it's really hard to search for it in Google because typing typing female or male on the plug doesn't yield you the correct results.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.600
I narrowed it down to it's brother component.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.040
Its the Kykon KPPX, or the KPPJ

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.740
Uuuhh (laughs) 
Rant complete

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.980
I ordered a piece that might work it might not

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.720
These are solder wires

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:29.760
Soldering Iron

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.600
And I need to solder plugs on the end of the wire, so I can plug it in

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:39.060
If you're ever buying a soldering iron, get one that has a temperature controlled head.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.400
Its basically one that has a thermostat in it

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.180
It will hold the heat you need all the time

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.120
It will never overheat and burn the solder on the end

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.300
It will never underheat, it just holds the perfect temperature.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.660
Its $40 compared to the usual like $10 or $20 you might pay

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:50.940
Its worth it, its worth every penny

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:54.200
I have to really be careful that these don't touch

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:55.920
And short each other out

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:56.760
That would be bad news

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.100
Let's be a little extra safe

00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.940
So I put some heat-shrink tubing on the end of this

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.440
(vroom)

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.580
and turn on this hair dryer

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.640
(vrooming intensifies)

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.360
Can you hold this wire for me?

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.280
Now the wire might get hot okay

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.080
(Nick: Okay, should I get gloves?)

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:12.080
No

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.380
Cool, I'll take this from you now

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.580
(screwdriver noises)

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:33.940
(vigorous popping)

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:37.780
(squeak)

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:38.540
(squeak)

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:44.360
The problem with talking about this stuff

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:46.540
like when I talk about film techniques

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:50.520
Is that I know what I'm talking about when I'm talking about VFX and film techniques

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.060
Where as I don't know

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.960
I don't know what I'm talking about when I talk about this

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.300
And I'm still figuring it out as I go so its like

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:02.940
I have long pauses of silence while I think about how I'm going to solve a problem

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.860
Such as, here's the plug that I've been looking for

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.960
for a loooooong time, finally found it

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.080
It cost like $10 just to order this one

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.080
The bottom is positive, the top is negative

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.440
(Cmike: what color is positive?)
Red

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.100
And its split across 2 pins because

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:25.280
the power draw from the laptop is so big that you need to get a 4 pin connector and just have 2 of the pins be the same thing for each half of it

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:26.340
And now

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.600
Now I need to solder this 10 gauge wire

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.280
to these pins, which are definitely not 10 gauge soldering pins

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:36.460
and I can't overlap it

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.420
I should probably start by just putting some solder in it.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:47.840
There's a little bit of

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:53.060
solder on your soldering iron, a little bit of solder on your connectors, and a little bit of solder on your wire

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:54.180
thats the best way to do it

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:58.620
I've gotta bridge both of these bottom pins with this wire, which I can do like this

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.120
But I can't have it touch the top pins

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.420
Its almost like I should just solder it to the wire like that

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.560
get a bunch of solder, thats all really toxic smoke, and heat up the wires

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:15.080
and the wires that get hot should wick away the metal from my soldering iron

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:21.340
this isn't like, exciting, huge problems here, this is just

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:23.140
This is just care and attention

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:31.040
But if there are any mistakes here, if I screw this up, than I got a long time to wait before I get my next connector

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:32.520
(Cmike: Im surprised you didn't order two)

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.500
Yeah, that's not the kind of guy I am

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.200
This is way better than a wet sponge

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:15.500
Freddie introduced me to this

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:18.220
Its just like a steel wool kind of thing with a bunch of like solder flux in it

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:22.440
and then all your solder from the tip of the iron falls of into the dish

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:26.580
it does a way better job of cleaning the iron than a wet sponge does

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:35.760
(vroom)

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.620
Now for the other side, ha ha ha

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.000
stripping a wire

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:12.020
Something about melting metal makes me feel really cool

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:13.840
And now I have to solder

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:16.920
If you can look here, if you get above it you can see there's the pin coming up

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.160
and then I'm just gonna do this

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:44.960
(vroom)

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.460
This plug, I think I did a good job

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:53.180
I need to put an XT60 connector on the other end of that wire

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:55.420
Specifically female

00:06:55.720 --> 00:07:01.160
And this is the part where you can't forget to put the heat shrink tubing on the wire

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:04.140
before you solder the stuff on it

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.380
This makes it a lot easier to connect the two because  all I have to do is heat them up and stick them together

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:19.180
And all I have to do here is squeeze it down

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:24.520
and once the heat transfers to the wire, it should get down to the solder I put in there and melt

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:28.320
Over here

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:29.080
whoops

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:29.960
(inhales)

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:32.120
Oh, its just the pin twists inside of it

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:41.400
(vroom)

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:45.380
So Jake is good at carpentry and wood craftsmanship

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.940
but I feel like I've gotten pretty good at soldering and wire-work

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.980
(Cmike: You two could build a house together)

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:53.520
Jake could build the frames, and I could put in the electrical

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:56.720
You see, being an electrician would be fun, I would actually enjoy being an electrician

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:05.200
That was so clean, it was the cleanest connection I've done yet.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:07.680
Its like sitting right in there

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:13.080
It is. Your eyes do not deceive you, it is that connector

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:14.900
(Wren: I knew it.)

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:18.380
(vroom)

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:20.080
Its all done!

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:25.100
I can plug a battery in, and I can plug it in to a laptop.

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:29.160
(Wren: Its pretty sweet)

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:33.100
Here's the thing, I can't dial in the amperage until I plug it in to the laptop.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:36.140
Um, hey, you're leaving right now aren't you

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:37.520
(Wren: I'm takin off, yeah, why is that?)

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:38.200
Umm

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.120
This is done, I could try it

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:44.940
(Wren: I- ok, you can try it)

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:48.820
Do you want to wait like 5 minutes for me to go get the laptop and the battery?

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:52.100
